meta {
  name: Unauthorized session
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/interface/modules/custom_modules/oe-module-clinical-copilot/public/ajax.php
  body: formUrlEncoded
  auth: none
}

body:form-urlencoded {
  csrf_token: not-a-real-token
  question: What active problems does this patient have?
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 403
  res.body.error: eq Session expired. Reload the page and try again.
}

docs {
  "Unauthorized user" variant, verified against a running instance: a
  request carrying a CSRF token that does not match the logged-in session's
  key -- the same failure shape a stale/expired session or a request replayed
  outside its originating session produces -- is rejected before ACL,
  patient scoping, or any co-pilot work runs:
  {"error":"Session expired. Reload the page and try again.","correlationId":"..."}

  This collection intentionally does not assume a second, ACL-restricted
  user account exists in every environment this runs against (local docker
  vs. Railway may seed different demo users) -- an invalid CSRF token
  against the real logged-in session is a portable way to exercise the same
  controller-level rejection path (CopilotChatController::buildResponse()'s
  CSRF check, which runs before its ACL check) without depending on
  environment-specific user data. If this environment does have a second,
  intentionally unprivileged user, logging in as that user instead and
  reusing this same request is an equally valid way to exercise the ACL
  check specifically.
}
